Root cause:
The bug was caused by improper management of memory related to link and network structures, specifically failing to remove a link from its master's bond slaves when the link was freed. This could lead to invalid memory access if the freed link was subsequently accessed through the master link's bond slaves.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
HASHMAP_FOREACH (carrier, link->bound_to_links, i)
    hashmap_remove(link->bound_to_links, INT_TO_PTR(carrier->ifindex));
HASHMAP_FOREACH (carrier, link->bound_by_links, i)
    hashmap_remove(link->bound_by_links, INT_TO_PTR(carrier->ifindex));
```
Reason:
These statements remove links from the `bound_to_links` and `bound_by_links` hashmaps without properly managing the `bond_slaves` of the master link. If a link is freed while still being referenced in its master's `bond_slaves`, it can lead to invalid memory access when the master attempts to access the freed link.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (link->network) {
    if (link->network->bond &&
        link_get(link->manager, link->network->bond->ifindex, &master) >= 0)
        (void) hashmap_remove(master->bond_slaves, INT_TO_PTR(link->ifindex));
}
```
Reason:
These statements ensure that before the link is freed, it is properly removed from the `bond_slaves` of its master link. This prevents any invalid memory access that could occur if the freed link is accessed through the master link's `bond_slaves`, thus maintaining proper memory management and integrity of the data structures.


